 "article": {
  "title": "Living in a Smarter City",
  "topic": "科技·城市生活",
  "level": "四级",
  "paras": [
   "More than half of the world's population now lives in cities, and by 2050 that share is expected to rise to nearly seven in ten. As cities grow, they face a familiar set of problems: crowded roads, polluted air, and public services that struggle to keep up. Many governments are turning to technology for answers, hoping that data can make city life smoother, safer and greener.",
   "The idea behind the smart city is simple. Sensors placed in streets, buses and buildings collect information about traffic, energy use and air quality. All of this data is sent to a central platform, where computer programs look for patterns. If a road is always jammed at eight in the morning, the city may adjust the timing of its traffic lights. If a bus route runs almost empty, it may be redesigned. In some districts, streetlights dim themselves when nobody is around, saving both money and electricity.",
   "Yet technology alone does not make a city wise. Critics point out that data can be misused, that cameras may watch citizens too closely, and that poorer neighborhoods are often the last to enjoy new services. A smart city, they argue, must be built on trust: people need to know what is being collected, who can see it, and how it will be used. Some cities now publish open data and invite residents to help decide which projects should come first.",
   "The most successful examples treat technology as a tool rather than a goal. The real question is not how many sensors a city owns, but whether its people live better because of them. When a new system saves a commuter twenty minutes, cuts a family's energy bill, or helps an elderly resident call for help, it earns its place. In the end, a city is smart only when it listens — to its data, and above all, to the people who live in it."
  ],
  "hard": [
   {
    "s": "More than half of the world's population now lives in cities, and by 2050 that share is expected to rise to nearly seven in ten.",
    "cn": "世界上已有超过一半的人口居住在城市，而到 2050 年这一比例预计将升至近七成。",
    "pt": "“七成”用 seven in ten 表达；“that share”指代前半句的“一半以上”这一比例。"
   },
   {
    "s": "A smart city, they argue, must be built on trust: people need to know what is being collected, who can see it, and how it will be used.",
    "cn": "他们认为，智慧城市必须建立在信任之上：人们需要知道哪些数据被采集、谁能看到这些数据、以及数据将如何被使用。",
    "pt": "they argue 是插入语；冒号后三个 what/who/how 从句并列，是典型的“排比式解释”。"
   },
